success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Normal period:** Under **Rule 485(a)(2)**, a post-effective amendment adding a new series to an existing trust normally becomes effective **75 days after filing**, unless the SEC accelerates or the registrant delays it. Most other 485(a) amendments use **60 days** under 485(a)(1).\n\n**Why it isn't a launch guarantee:**\n- SEC staff comments may require changes or a delay.\n- Final terms usually go in a **Rule 485(b)** filing, and the 75-day date can be pushed back with 485(a)(3)/(b)(1)(iii) amendments.\n- Launch still needs board approvals, exchange listing under Rule 6c-11 generic standards (or Rule 19b-4 if non-generic), and a Form 8-A.\n- Operational steps include CUSIP, seed capital, authorized participants, custody, index licensing and service-provider contracts.\n\n**New trust:** It files Form N-8A and an initial Form N-1A under Securities Act §8(a). No 485 timeline applies. Effectiveness is nominally 20 days, but filers almost always add a Rule 473 delaying amendment. Effectiveness then depends on SEC review and acceleration, which often takes months.","ttft_ms":5184,"type":"result","duration_ms":9237,"uuid":"906e2931-e675-4148-a391-34c88b5deb63","ttft_stream_ms":1722,"time_to_request_ms":60,"first_content_frame_ms":1722,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
